---
term: Secession
section: evolution-and-protection
order: 110
definition: The withdrawal of a subsidiary charter or a faction from its parent, together with a share of the parent's commons.
seeAlso:
  - recourse
  - exit
  - jurisdiction
---
Secession is the [[exit]] of a group instead of an individual. Because the group takes part of the commons with it, secession also changes the parent's [[jurisdiction]].

A parent's articles or a [[contract]] can set the terms of secession in advance, such as which share of the commons goes with the seceding party. Without such terms, secession is settled by struggle.
